sandbox

# Example flows copied in for a trial run.
#
# Both runners ship flows/ empty on purpose — flows are yours. Trying the
# examples means copying them in from examples/example-runners/ (CI does exactly
# this, which is what keeps them compiling against the current runner), and
# without these lines every trial run leaves the tree full of untracked files
# that are really just a copy of something already committed elsewhere.
#
# Note for anyone using this repo *as* their runner rather than copying it out:
# your own Go flows would land here too. Either track them with `git add -f`, or
# work outside the repo, which is what the Readmes now recommend.
/go/flows/*.go
!/go/flows/flows.go
/python/flows/auto_registered/

# noCRUD perf: run artifacts are transient; the baseline is committed
python/perf/runs/
go/perf/runs/

# Compiled runner binaries. `go build ./cmd/<x>` drops the binary in the working
# directory under the command's own name.
#
# A gitignore pattern cannot say "the file, not the directory", and go/nocrud is
# both — the binary AND the core package. Without the negation below, the
# package source is silently never staged and the runner does not build from a
# clean checkout. The other four are only ever binaries (their sources live in
# go/cmd/), and these patterns are anchored to the repo root, so go/cmd/nocrud
# is unaffected either way.
/go/nocrud
!/go/nocrud/
/go/perfreport
/go/createcrudflow
/go/modelcoverage
/go/initdb
